Module visibility determines which declarations can cross a module boundary. Shared helper declarations are available within their owning module. Other modules can see only the names published by the applicable `@mod.moth` or `@package.moth` facade using `$export`.

### Shared helpers and public facades

A rooted directory is one module. Its ordinary `.moth` files, including files in unrooted subdirectories, share declarations through dependency clauses.

[codeblock, $code "text":
    @entry.moth
    components/
    ├── @mod.moth
    └── utils.moth
]

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    -- components/utils.moth
    internal_value #= 42
]

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    -- components/@mod.moth
    $export
    @utils internal_value

    $export @forms
    form_label #= "Form"
]

The facade imports and publishes `internal_value` at its root and
`form_label` under the `forms` route. Another module can now use:

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    @components internal_value
    @components/forms form_label
]

Without `$export`, the same `@utils` dependency would be private to the facade. Other modules cannot bypass the child facade using `@components/utils`.

### Public route visibility

A facade may publish names at its root and under public routes. Each route is a
public namespace below the facade, and it is visible exactly where that facade
is visible.

`@components form_label` fails because `form_label` is not a root member.
`@components/forms internal_value` fails because the route does not publish it.
A bare route namespace is shallow: `@components/forms as forms` exposes
`forms.form_label` and nothing from the facade root or any deeper route.
Recursive access such as `html.tables.cells` is unsupported, so `@core/html as
html` does not enable it.

A private directory inside the exporting module may share a route spelling. It
stays private. `@components/forms hidden_value` finds only an exported
`hidden_value` in that route, never a file under `components/forms/`. An
undeclared route or leaf reports an import diagnostic rather than falling
through to the private directory.

Concrete route overlaps are checked eagerly. A route that overlaps a visible
child facade or one of its routes, a source or child namespace key or a
registered source or binding package prefix reports `MOTH-IMPORT-0006`, even
when no consumer references it. A case-only match counts. Two facades that share
one visible key create an ambiguous base: identical route claims from different
facades behind that base stay latent and the base and each such route report
`MOTH-IMPORT-0006` on use. A route behind an ambiguous base that overlaps a
different visible provider, such as a selectable child facade or one of its
routes, a local source or a registered package prefix, is instead an eager
`MOTH-IMPORT-0006` collision even when unused. Case-only route conflicts inside
one facade report `MOTH-RULE-0092` at both operands.

Registered source-package routes sit beneath the registered package path, and
scoped project-local support routes sit beneath the support package name. A
project-root package facade records its routes and interface in package assembly
metadata for publication, but general project-package acquisition and
consumption remain deferred. Registered source packages and scoped project-local
support packages are the supported route providers. The `.mtf` prelude stays
root-only, so a route never adds a prelude name.

### Root-local declarations

Declarations authored directly in `@mod.moth`, `@package.moth` or `@entry.moth` belong to that root's own scope. They are not shared with other roots or ordinary helpers. A facade can publish its own declarations, but a sibling root cannot import or re-export them.

Shared code belongs in ordinary helper files. Each root may depend on those helpers through normal dependency clauses. Same-spelling declarations in separate roots are independent unless an imported helper introduces a name collision.

Direct dependency paths to root filenames or `@config.moth` are invalid. Depend on the module directory or a shared source file instead. See @../project-structure/#module-roots (Module roots).

### Separate public surfaces

`@mod.moth` publishes the normal module API. `@package.moth` publishes a scoped support package API or the external project package API at the project root. `@entry.moth` has no importable public surface and only its selected entry executes.

A dependency selects the surface appropriate to its consumer. It cannot fall back to the other facade. A child with only `@entry.moth` has no normal public API. Support packages have additional scoped consumer restrictions, described in @#project-local-packages (Project-local packages).

A facade with no `$export` declarations or clauses has an empty public API. Routes are part of that API, not a separate facade. A cross-module import of a private name is rejected regardless of the path used.

### Methods and public contracts

Receiver methods follow the visibility of their nominal receiver type. A caller with a value of a public receiver type can call its methods without separately importing that type name. Naming the type in a signature, constructor or conformance still requires a suitable type binding. Methods cannot be imported or exported individually. A routed receiver type carries its methods, but a method is not a route leaf.

Public signatures must expose only accessible types and trait evidence. Exported callables without an error slot must handle any escaping implicit built-in failure. The same guarantee applies to exposed methods, re-exports and routed exports. See @../project-structure/#public-api (Public API).

### Dependency ordering

A dependency clause binds names throughout its file, independent of source position. File dependency cycles inside one module are allowed when declarations can be resolved in dependency order. Circular constant dependencies are rejected.

